          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-0bd9dd71e718a28a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The speaker directly and retrospectively avows that he loved Lora, presenting his painful love as the motive for fleeing to avoid harming her. The utterance is chiefly truth-conditional: it asserts a past emotional state and explains conduct. It neither undertakes a present relational commitment nor shows positive evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal is rhetorically self-vindicating and accusatory, so its sincerity may be questionable, but that does not alter its strongly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A direct spoken retrospective avowal embedded in the construction “io, che t’amavo” (“I, who loved you”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"In a tense confrontation with his sister Lora, the man invokes his former anguished love and his decision to flee as a defense of his conduct and an implicit reproach concerning her relationship with Rafa. His murmured delivery, distress, and rhetorical question intensify the avowal but do not independently make it expressive/reflexive. Any manipulation, self-dramatization, or doubtful sincerity belongs to the context rather than requiring O.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Sì, Lora, io, che t’amavo con tanta pena” (“Yes, Lora, I, who loved you with such anguish”).\",\"supports\":\"A direct, explicit presentation of his past loving emotional state strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“io, che fuggivo disperatamente per non farti male?” (“I, who fled desperately so as not to hurt you?”).\",\"supports\":\"The love avowal is used to explain and justify a past action, reinforcing its assertive, truth-conditional role rather than establishing a new undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The speaker murmurs the statement after a silence, within an extended confrontation about Lora becoming Rafa’s mistress.\",\"supports\":\"The calculated retrospective and rhetorical framing weighs against treating the words as a reflex-like verbal discharge; the emotional delivery alone does not support E.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative imperfect “t’amavo,” with past tense/aspect.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the core expression presents an ongoing past state of loving rather than explicitly making a present commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the retrospective avowal; the reproachful, self-justifying context can be described separately without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The passage involves an incestuous sibling relationship, but that transgressive context does not change the core utterance’s semantic-pragmatic classification.\"}"
        }
